//! @yah:gotcha("THE DEV RAFT GROUP STRUCTURALLY CANNOT PUBLISH A ROUTABLE SERVICE RECORD, AND CONTAINER NETWORKING DOES NOT FIX IT. Found 2026-09-14 running 'yah cloud apply --env staging --service noisetable-api' from the noisetable camp immediately after the us-west-011 roll recorded above. The apply FAILED at ingress upstream resolution (not at the deploy, and not on a Cloudflare token): 'no ready service record for noisetable-account-staging'. CHAIN, EVERY LINK READ IN SOURCE, NOT INFERRED. (1) 011's live record is {mesh_ip: 127.0.0.1, ports: [4332], endpoints: [127.0.0.1:4332], health: not-ready, reason: unroutable}, so GET /service-records?ready=true returns an EMPTY set and ingress.rs's resolve_upstreams errors by design. (2) 127.0.0.1 is PROOF that binds_node_ports(spec) is TRUE for this spec, not merely consistent with it: workload_bind_ip (lib.rs:2170) is the only producer of LOCALHOST — 'if binds_node_ports(spec) { return Some(self.node_mesh_ip.unwrap_or(LOCALHOST)) }' — while the other branch, allocate_container_address, is 'let node = self.node_mesh_ip?' (lib.rs:2198) and yields None, i.e. NO address, never loopback. The spec has [expose.public] port = 4332, so it short-circuits before allocate_container_address is ever reached. CONSEQUENCE: W343 container networking is IRRELEVANT to this workload and could never have unblocked it. Today's 011 roll is correct capability and this ticket still wanted it, but it was not the blocker. (3) The loopback is NOT stale-and-sticky — I checked, because that was my first theory and it is wrong: refreshed_mesh_ip (service_records.rs:1652) explicitly excludes Ipv4Addr::LOCALHOST from its preserve branch, so a 127.0.0.1 record is re-derived every refresh rather than pinned. It comes back loopback because the derivation itself yields loopback. (4) ROOT CAUSE: node_mesh_ip is None on 011. Its ONLY setter is ServerState::with_bind_addr (lib.rs:1766), 'self.node_mesh_ip = parse_node_mesh_ip(bind)' — derived solely from --bind, with no flag and no env override anywhere in the tree. parse_node_mesh_ip (lib.rs:1494) returns None for UNSPECIFIED by explicit test. 011's effective ExecStart, read from systemctl show, is '--bind 0.0.0.0:7443', set by yubaba.service.d/40-raft.conf — the R608-F18 drop-in that exists PRECISELY so the dev group answers on both its LAN and its mesh address. So the bind that makes the dev group reachable is the same bind that makes node_mesh_ip None. This is structural for ALL THREE of us-west-011/013/014, not a misconfiguration of 011; 013 and 014 simply hold zero records today so it has never surfaced there. CONTRAST, measured the same minute: us-east-001 binds its mesh IP, node_mesh_ip = 100.64.0.3, and all its records are health:ready — including noisetable-account at mesh_ip 10.128.3.2, the R881-T5 canary's container address. THREE WAYS OUT, ranked. (a) MATURE FIX, and the only one that fixes the group rather than one service: give yubaba a mesh-address source independent of --bind (a --mesh-ip / YUBABA_MESH_IP, or read the tailscale interface), since --bind and 'what address do I advertise' are two different questions that this code conflates. Note lib.rs:412's standing @yah:next already circles this function for R881-S2. (b) ESCAPE HATCH, what the apply's own error suggests: pin upstream_host = \"100.64.0.10\" + port = 4332 on [providers.compute] in the noisetable camp's staging.toml. One line, unblocks today, but hardcodes an address the control plane is meant to discover — exactly the declared-vs-live drift ingress.rs:812 warns about ('the TOML is the one that can be stale'). (c) DO NOT rebind the dev group to its mesh IP. Its raft members advertise 192.168.10.11|13|14:7443 (read from /raft/status) and a mesh-only bind would partition the group. NOTHING WAS BROKEN BY THE FAILED APPLY: api-staging.noisetable.com still answers 200/216 from the production passway edge and its DNS is unchanged (51.81.85.145, 45.32.194.254). Deserves its own ticket; filed here because this is where the session's context lives.")
//! @yah:handoff("R881-S2 FIX LANDED IN THE TREE (uncommitted; camp git policy is defer). Gives yubaba a mesh-address source independent of --bind, per the operator call taken 2026-09-14 on the finding recorded in the gotcha above. SHAPE, and why it is not a second source beside --bind: ServerState::with_bind_addr(bind) is RENAMED to with_mesh_addr(explicit: Option<Ipv4Addr>, bind: &str) with body 'self.node_mesh_ip = explicit.or_else(|| parse_node_mesh_ip(bind))'. That keeps ONE setter for the field and puts the precedence in exactly one place, rather than adding a with_node_mesh_ip that could disagree with the bind derivation — the 'read both and prefer whichever is present' shape root CLAUDE.md names as the thing that becomes a research project three months later. New '--mesh-ip' flag on 'yubaba serve', 'env = \"YUBABA_MESH_IP\"' matching --container-net's idiom EXACTLY and for the same recorded reason: a fleet node's ExecStart= is already re-declared by the raft drop-in, so Environment= composes where a second ExecStart= silently drops flags. parse_node_mesh_ip is now pub and is what the BINARY validates --mesh-ip through — deliberately one parser for both inputs, so an explicit address and a derived one cannot drift on what counts as dialable. A malformed or undialable --mesh-ip FAILS STARTUP rather than falling back to the derived address, matching the --container-net precedent set by R881-T4: an operator who named an address asked for that one. CALL SITES: 11 total, 1 production (main.rs) + 10 test, across lib.rs, tests/integration_service_records.rs and tests/integration_deploy_through_kamaji.rs; every one updated, plus three stale prose references to the old name (lib.rs:320 and :346's @yah: annotations about cloudflared_url, and a doc comment in integration_deploy_through_kamaji.rs). DELIBERATELY NOT UPDATED: the two with_bind_addr mentions in .yah/docs/working/W267-sovereign-public-ingress.md, which are that ticket's historical file manifest and are accurate about what W267 shipped. NEW TEST: an_explicit_mesh_address_overrides_what_the_bind_flag_would_derive (lib.rs, bundle_deploy_tests). It pins the WILDCARD case first, because that is the actual bug — a 0.0.0.0 bind plus an explicit address must advertise the address — then that explicit beats a derivable bind, then that absent an explicit one the derivation is untouched (the property every already-deployed node relies on), then that the parser takes the BARE address shape the flag actually receives and rejects 0.0.0.0/127.0.0.1/localhost/::1/garbage. That last leg matters because the bare-IP branch of parse_node_mesh_ip was previously only reachable from the derivation path.")
