//! @yah:gotcha("R881-S2 SHIP DEFERRED 2026-09-14 BY OPERATOR, AND THE REASON IS A PRECONDITION, NOT A PREFERENCE — check it before you pick this up. scripts/hotship.sh builds THE WORKING TREE by its own design ('the deliberate opposite of roll-node.sh', its header), so on this one-working-tree camp a hot ship carries whatever every OTHER live session has uncommitted at that moment, not just your own change. At the moment this was parked, two peers were mid-edit: the qed sweep adding yah_qed::Pipeline::allow_late_operator_block (root workspace red with E0063 at app/yah/cli/src/camp.rs:10993 and crates/yah/agent-tools/src/agent_dispatch_tools.rs:10627), and a second session inside oss/yubaba/crates/cloud/src/reconciler/ — a hotship build's own skew verdict caught 10 changed inputs there mid-run, including ingress.rs, domain.rs, ingress_verify.rs and mesofact_bundle.rs. ingress.rs is the exact file whose resolve_upstreams this whole track is trying to satisfy, so shipping across that edit would have put an unknown half-state of someone else's work onto a raft voter and made any result unattributable. This is R881-T5's 'the fleet is running code that exists nowhere in git' hazard reached by a different road. WHAT TO RE-CHECK BEFORE RESUMING, in order: (1) 'git status --short oss/yubaba oss/qed' quiet or at least understood; (2) 'cargo check --workspace --all-targets' from the repo root green, which is what proves the qed sweep finished; (3) re-run 'cargo test -p yubaba --lib' from oss/yubaba and confirm it still passes on the settled tree rather than trusting this ticket's 946/0, which was measured on the contended one; (4) then 'scripts/hotship.sh --nodes us-west-011 --binaries kamaji,yubaba --dry-run' — it builds, resolves and probes without touching the node, and is the cheap way to find out whether the pair still builds before a voter is involved. NODE STATE WHILE PARKED IS COHERENT, not half-applied: us-west-011 has the container-net drop-ins live and ip_forward persisted (the roll recorded above), runs a matched 0.8.40-h4 kamaji/yubaba pair, is a raft FOLLOWER behind leader 14 at term 6, and its noisetable-account-staging record still reads 127.0.0.1/unroutable exactly as it did before — because the --mesh-ip that fixes that is tree-only and not installed. api-staging.noisetable.com is untouched and still answers 200/216 from the production passway edge; its DNS was never wired and MUST NOT BE until a connector is actually up.")
//! @yah:gotcha("I WAS WRONG ABOUT binds_node_ports, AND THE CORRECTION INVERTS AN EARLIER CONCLUSION ON THIS TICKET — read this before the gotcha above that says container networking is irrelevant to noisetable-account-staging. That claim is FALSE and is disproved by the live record. MEASURED after the R881-S2 ship (2026-09-14, us-west-011 on 0.8.40-h6): the record reads mesh_ip 10.128.10.2, health READY, and GET /service-records?ready=true returns it. 10.128.10.2 is a CONTAINER address out of this node's derived 10.128.10.0/24 — it can only come from allocate_container_address, i.e. from the binds_node_ports == FALSE branch. WHERE MY REASONING BROKE, stated so the error is not repeatable rather than just retracted. I claimed the observed 127.0.0.1 was PROOF that binds_node_ports was true, on the grounds that workload_bind_ip is the only producer of LOCALHOST. It is not. service_records.rs:1231 is 'let mesh_ip = mesh_ip.unwrap_or(Ipv4Addr::LOCALHOST)' — a SECOND localhost fallback, at record construction, which materialises 'no address' into a record whose mesh_ip field is a bare Ipv4Addr and cannot hold None. I asserted a sole producer without enumerating the producers. Separately, and decisively, binds_node_ports (service_records.rs:511) is 'wants_host_network() || wants_native_exec() || annotations.contains_key(\"yah.docker.publish\")' — [expose.public] appears NOWHERE in it, so the spec's port = 4332 never made that predicate true. I inferred it from a value instead of reading the four-line function. THE CORRECT CHAIN: binds_node_ports false -> allocate_container_address -> 'let node = self.node_mesh_ip?' -> None while the bind was 0.0.0.0 -> record construction turns None into 127.0.0.1 -> unroutable. Root cause is UNCHANGED and was right: node_mesh_ip was None because --bind was a wildcard. CONSEQUENCE FOR THE ROLL ORDER, which is the part that matters operationally: the two changes COMPOSE and neither alone suffices. --mesh-ip supplies node_mesh_ip; --container-net supplies the range whose /24 allocate_container_address carves the workload's address out of. With only --mesh-ip the node would have had an address to derive from and no range to allocate in; with only --container-net (the state this ticket left us-west-011 in for three hours) there was a range and no node address to derive the /24 from. So the container-net roll recorded above was LOAD-BEARING for this workload, not the capability-in-advance the earlier entry called it. The generalisation worth keeping: on this node class a workload is routable only when BOTH KAMAJI_CONTAINER_NET/YUBABA_CONTAINER_NET and a resolvable node mesh address are present.")
