//! @yah:handoff("PART 2 — THE NEW BLOCKER, WHICH IS REAL PROGRESS AND A DIFFERENT BUG. The refusal message CHANGED, which is how we know the helper is now wired and actually executing. BEFORE: `BackendRefused: workload headscale declares yah.durability.tier = \"stream\" but this kamaji has no hydrate helper configured`. NOW (journalctl -u yubaba on us-south-001, 20:25:53 and every retry round since): `BackendRefused: workload headscale was not hydrated and must not start: {\"outcome\":\"error\",\"message\":\"S3_ACCESS_KEY is required: environment variable not found\"}`. kamaji found the helper, ran it, and the helper refused for lack of object-store credentials. GROUNDED CAUSE: hydrate.rs's `run()` passes VOLUME_ROOT / SUBJECTS / TIER / OWNER / S3_BUCKET / BACKUP_PREFIX to the helper from the spec, but its own comment states credentials and endpoint \"are inherited from kamaji's own environment (S3_ACCESS_KEY / S3_SECRET_KEY / S3_ENDPOINT / S3_REGION) rather than set here. kamaji never reads them, so they do not pass through a supervisor that has no business holding them.\" turso-backup's hydrate binary requires them (oss/turso-backup/src/bin/hydrate.rs:35-40; the same contract in tail.rs:307-311). AND NOTHING PROVISIONS THEM ONTO KAMAJI: `systemctl show kamaji.service -p Environment` names exactly five variables, none matching S3_*, and kamaji's unit and drop-ins carry NO EnvironmentFile at all. The 50-durability-helpers.conf that control_plane_install.sh:181-191 writes sets only the two helper PATHS — by construction it never carried credentials. On this node the R2 credentials live in /etc/yah-cloud/cert-store.env, referenced by yubaba.service.d/50-cert-store.conf as `EnvironmentFile=/etc/yah-cloud/cert-store.env` (yubaba also references `-/etc/yah-cloud/litestream.env`, which is the more likely right source since litestream already replicates this very headscale.db). I did NOT wire either one — that is a credential-placement decision with a real blast radius (it puts object-store secrets into the supervisor that forks every workload on the box, which is precisely the property hydrate.rs's comment is reasoning about), and the ticket's boundary said stop rather than improvise.")
